        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<p>
Among the investigations that have grown plants in space, none have claimed to have
achieved optimal conditions for plant growth. It is believed the most crucial components
holding back major advancements in this research are providing adequate levels of water
and oxygen to the root zone. Previous studies utilizing other hardware designs have found
that the surface tension of water under microgravity conditions has often been found to
lead to either severe water-logging or excessive drying in the root zone. Consequently,
differences in plant growth responses between space flight experiments and their ground
controls occur based merely upon differences in moisture distribution patterns between the
two conditions. Until a better means of controlling these critical aspects of plant
culture are developed, results of all investigations will be called into question. The
Microgravity Plant Nutrient Experiment (MPNE) hardware was designed to address these
concerns.<br>
<br>
The primary objective of this test was to demonstrate the potential use of porous tubes to
provide water and essential minerals to higher plants grown in microgravity. This was
accomplished by: (1) assessing whether the Water Availability Sensors and Water Delivery
System could work in concert to maintain a predetermined volume of nutrient solution at
the root zone, (2) collecting and archiving system performance data from various sensors
for post-flight analysis of system operation, and (3) automatically imbibing and
germinating seeds on-orbit.<p>APPROACH:<p>
A Hardware Verification Test (HVT) of MPNE was conducted on STS-89 in the Space Shuttles
Middeck. As a biological indicator of hardware performance, wheat (Triticum aestivum cv
Yecora rojo) seeds were flown. The seeds were loaded dry three days prior to Orbiter
lift-off. Environmental conditions were set to 80-100% relative humidity and 15-35 degrees
C. The system was initiated by the crew after three days on-orbit.<p>RESULTS:<p>
Several hours after the system was initiated a failure of the Water Availability Sensor
(WAS) resulted in the inability of the fluid circuit to adequately manage the plant
nutrient solution for the plant tubes. MPNE requires that the delivery of water to roots
grown in microgravity be done in a precisely controlled fashion. This involves the
non-invasive detection and monitoring of a thin-film of the nutrient solution on the
surface of the porous tubes by the WAS and the subsequent active control of this
"wetness level" via a "fuzzy-logic" feedback system. When the WAS
failed to perform this function early in the mission, the system switched to the back-up
operational mode where the calculated reservoir volume was divided into time specific
delivery volumes required to last for the missions duration, and these amounts (ca
65 microliters) were delivered once a minute. Ground tests have shown that when this
back-up mode was implemented (even at the start of an experiment), plant germination and
subsequent growth proceeds nominally. However, during the MPNE space flight test, without
gravity pulling downward on any excess nutrient solution being provided to the tubes, the
seed cylinders became flooded at the time of system initiation and consequently
germination failed to proceed. Hence, our water delivery rate in the fixed-feed mode was
incorrect for the pattern of water distribution as it occurred in microgravity. Despite
most of the MPNE subsystems functioning nominally, the failure of the WAS (although
followed by a successful automatic switch to the back-up, fixed-feed, mode of operation)
coupled with this excessive water delivery characteristic (in microgravity) of the back-up
control method, resulted in the flooding of the seeds. <br>
<br>
</p>
